
/**
 * Definition for singly-linked list.
 * Each node contains a value and a pointer to the next node.
 */
public class ListNode {
    int val;        // value stored in the node
    ListNode next;  // pointer to next node

    ListNode() {}   // default constructor

    ListNode(int val) { 
        this.val = val; 
    }

    ListNode(int val, ListNode next) { 
        this.val = val; 
        this.next = next; 
    }
}

class Solution {
    public ListNode addTwoNumbers(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {

        // Head will store the start of the result linked list
        ListNode head = null;

        // Temp pointer is used to move through the result list
        ListNode temp = null;

        // Carry value for addition (like normal digit addition)
        int carry = 0;

        // Loop continues while:
        // l1 has nodes OR l2 has nodes OR carry still exists
        while(l1 != null || l2 != null || carry != 0){

            // If l1 becomes null, use 0 as its value
            int val1 = (l1 == null) ? 0 : l1.val;

            // If l2 becomes null, use 0 as its value
            int val2 = (l2 == null) ? 0 : l2.val;

            // Add both values and carry
            int value = val1 + val2 + carry;

            // Create new node with last digit of sum
            // (value % 10 gives the unit digit)
            ListNode newNode = new ListNode(value % 10);

            // Update carry for next iteration
            // (value / 10 gives carry digit)
            carry = value / 10;

            // If result list is empty, set head and temp to new node
            if(head == null){
                head = newNode;
                temp = newNode;
            }
            else{
                // Otherwise attach new node to result list
                temp.next = newNode;

                // Move temp pointer forward
                temp = temp.next;
            }

            // Move l1 pointer to next node if it exists
            if(l1 != null){
                l1 = l1.next;
            }

            // Move l2 pointer to next node if it exists
            if(l2 != null){
                l2 = l2.next;
            }
        }

        // Suggest garbage collection (not necessary in Java normally)
        System.gc();

        // Return head of the result linked list
        return head;
    }
}

💡 What this problem does

This is the famous LeetCode problem: Add Two Numbers.

Example:

l1 = [2 → 4 → 3]   (represents 342)
l2 = [5 → 6 → 4]   (represents 465)

Output:
[7 → 0 → 8]        (represents 807)

Because:

342 + 465 = 807

The digits are stored in reverse order in the linked list.

⚙️ Time & Space Complexity

Time Complexity: O(max(n, m))

Space Complexity: O(max(n, m)) (for result list)

